  "question": {
    "text": "Let $f$ and $g$ be two differentiable functions on $R$ such that $f^{\\prime}(x)>0$ and $g^{\\prime}(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in R$. Then for all $\\mathrm{x}$ :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "XgYRB",
      "text": "\n$f(g(x))>f(g(x-1))$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "BBzci",
      "text": "\n$f(g(x))>f(g(x+1))$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "ZnxN4",
      "text": "\n$g(f(x))>g(f(x-1))$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "UTtGy",
      "text": "\n$g(f(x)) < g(f(x+1))$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Since $f^{\\prime}(x)>0$ and $g^{\\prime}(x) < 0$, therefore $f(x)$ is increasing function and $g(x)$ is decreasing function.\n$\\Rightarrow f(x+1)>f(x)$ and $g(x+1) < g(x)$\n$\\Rightarrow g[f(x+1)] < g[f(x)]$ and $f[g(x+$\n1) $] < f[g(x)]$\nHence option (b) is correct.",
    "image": null
  },
